{"id":"d8c7b56a-42b5-4b54-a8a3-80cc5c08ff87","object":"chat.completion","created":1784801740,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    import copy\\n    payload = case.get('payload')\\n    if not payload:\\n        raise ValueError('Missing payload')\\n    items = payload.get('items')\\n    required_failure = payload.get('required_failure_items')\\n    unresolved_without = payload.get('unresolved_without')\\n    if not (isinstance(items, list) and isinstance(required_failure, list) and isinstance(unresolved_without, list)):\\n        raise ValueError('Invalid payload structure')\\n    def outcome(config):\\n        missing_unresolved = [x for x in unresolved_without if x not in config]\\n        if missing_unresolved:\\n            return 'UNRESOLVED'\\n        missing_required = [x for x in required_failure if x not in config]\\n        if not missing_required:\\n            return 'FAIL'\\n        return 'PASS'\\n    initial = list(items)\\n    if outcome(initial) != 'FAIL':\\n        raise ValueError('Initial configuration does not produce FAIL')\\n    current = initial[:]\\n    n = 2\\n    test_count = 0\\n    def test(config):\\n        nonlocal test_count\\n        test_count += 1\\n        return outcome(config)\\n    while True:\\n        if n >= len(current):\\n            break\\n        length = len(current)\\n        size = length // n\\n        remainder = length % n\\n        subsets = []\\n        start = 0\\n        for i in range(n):\\n            subset_size = size + (1 if i < remainder else 0)\\n            end = start + subset_size\\n            subsets.append(current[start:end])\\n            start = end\\n        found = False\\n        for subset in subsets:\\n            if test(subset) == 'FAIL':\\n                current = subset[:]\\n                n = 2\\n                found = True\\n                break\\n        if found:\\n            continue\\n        start = 0\\n        for i in range(n):\\n            subset_size = size + (1 if i < remainder else 0)\\n            end = start + subset_size\\n            complement = current[:start] + current[end:]\\n            if test(complement) == 'FAIL':\\n                current = complement[:]\\n                n = 2\\n                found = True\\n                break\\n            start = end\\n        if found:\\n            continue\\n        n = min(2 * n, length)\\n    return {\\n        'minimal_failure': current,\\n        'test_count': test_count,\\n        'terminal_outcome': 'FAIL'\\n    }\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement `solve(case)` that performs ddmin (delta debugging) with PASS, FAIL, UNRESOLVED semantics. The goal is to find a 1-minimal failure-inducing subset (ordered configuration) that still produces FAIL, given initial configuration (items list) that should produce FAIL. The algorithm: start with full set, test it (must be FAIL; if not, raise ValueError). Then recursively partition into n subsets (starting with 2), test each subset; if any subset is FAIL, reduce to that subset and continue with granularity 2; if none are FAIL but complement(s) are FAIL, reduce to complement(s) - but note: only FAIL permits reduction, PASS and UNRESOLVED do not. The algorithm description: \"Only FAIL permits reduction; PASS and UNRESOLVED do not. Decrease granularity after reduction and otherwise double it up to current size.\" Actually typical ddmin: start with n=2, test subsets; if any subset fails, set config to that subset and continue with n=2 (reset granularity). If no subset fails but complement fails, set config to complement and continue with n=2. If no subset fails and no complement fails (i.e., all subsets PASS or UNRESOLVED), then increase n (double it, but not exceeding current size) and repeat. Continue until n >= |config| (i.e., cannot split further) and no further reduction possible. Then result is current config, number of tests, and terminal outcome (should be FAIL). The spec says \"Partition at the current granularity and test subsets before complements.\" Also \"Only FAIL permits reduction; PASS and UNRESOLVED do not.\" So when testing a subset, if outcome is FAIL, we reduce to that subset and reset granularity to 2. If no subset is FAIL, then test complements? Actually the order: test subsets first, then if none FAIL, test complements of those subsets? But complement could be the rest of the items. The typical ddmin algorithm: for each subset, if subset fails -> reduce; if no subset fails, then for each complement (i.e., items not in subset), if complement fails -> reduce. But the spec says \"test subsets before complements.\" So we must test all subsets first; if any FAIL, reduce and continue. If none FAIL, then test complements? However, the spec also says \"Only FAIL permits reduction; PASS and UNRESOLVED do not.\" So PASS/UNRESOLVED subsets/complements are ignored. If no FAIL found among subsets or complements, then we double granularity and repeat (unless n already >= |config|). The final result should be a 1-minimal failure (i.e., removing any single item makes it not FAIL). The algorithm should guarantee that after termination, the configuration is 1-minimal (each item is necessary for failure). We need to implement the oracle: we have `case` with items list, required_failure_items, unresolved_without. The test function determines outcome based on the configuration (a list of items, presumably the current configuration we are testing). The oracle: \n- If the configuration includes all required_failure_items? Actually the semantics: \"Require the initial configuration to produce FAIL.\" So initial config is all items? The case payload includes items list, required_failure_items, unresolved_without. The test should produce FAIL if the configuration contains all required_failure_items? But there is also unresolved_without: if the configuration does not contain any item from unresolved_without? Actually \"unresolved_without\" means if those items are missing, the outcome is UNRESOLVED? Let's interpret: The description \"unresolved_without\" suggests that if the configuration does NOT include any of those items, then outcome is UNRESOLVED. Possibly also if configuration contains all required_failure_items? Actually the typical ddmin with PASS/FAIL/UNRESOLVED: The oracle gives FAIL if the configuration induces a failure (i.e., contains the failure-causing elements), PASS if not, and UNRESOLVED if the configuration is missing some necessary conditions (like required items) making it impossible to determine. Here the case provides required_failure_items (items that must be present for failure) and unresolved_without (items that if absent, make outcome UNRESOLVED). The initial configuration (all items) should be FAIL because it contains all required_failure_items and does not miss any unresolved_without items? Actually initial config includes all items, so it includes required_failure_items, so should be FAIL. But to be precise, we need to define the oracle function.\n\nFrom the fixture, there are three cases with different required_failure_items and unresolved_without. For case 000: required_failure_items [\"d2\",\"d3\"], unresolved_without []. So any configuration that includes both d2 and d3 should be FAIL? Possibly also if missing any of them, it's PASS? But what about unresolved? If unresolved_without is empty, then no UNRESOLVED condition. So outcome: if config contains both d2 and d3 => FAIL; else PASS? But there might be other constraints? The spec says \"external test execution is replaced by a deterministic oracle fixture.\" So we must implement oracle based on these fields. Likely: \n- If the configuration does not contain any item from \"unresolved_without\", then outcome is UNRESOLVED. (Because without those items, the test cannot be run).\n- Else, if the configuration contains all items in \"required_failure_items\", then outcome is FAIL.\n- Else outcome is PASS.\n\nBut also need to consider: what if configuration contains some but not all required_failure_items? Then PASS. What if configuration contains all required_failure_items but is missing some unresolved_without? Then it's UNRESOLVED (since unresolved condition takes precedence). The spec says: \"Only FAIL permits reduction; PASS and UNRESOLVED do not.\" So we need to implement deterministic outcome according to these rules.\n\nCheck case 001: required_failure_items [\"d3\",\"d4\"], unresolved_without [\"d0\"]. So initial config (all items) includes d3, d4, d0 => contains required_failure_items and contains d0 (so not missing unresolved_without) => FAIL. Good. Case 002: required_failure_items [\"d4\",\"d7\"], unresolved_without [\"d0\"] => initial includes all => FAIL.\n\nNow, what about subsets? If we test a subset that does not include d0, then outcome is UNRESOLVED (because missing unresolved_without). That cannot be used for reduction. If it includes d0 but not both d4 and d7, then PASS. So algorithm will skip UNRESOLVED and PASS.\n\nNow implement solve(case). We need to validate fields: case must have 'payload' with 'items', 'required_failure_items', 'unresolved_without'. Also ensure that initial configuration (all items) produces FAIL. If not, raise ValueError.\n\nDefine a function test(config) that returns 'FAIL', 'PASS', or 'UNRESOLVED' based on the rules.\n\nThen implement ddmin:\n- current = list of all items (copy).\n- n = 2 (granularity).\n- tests = 0 (count test calls).\n- outcome = test(current) -> must be FAIL, else error.\n- loop:\n  - if n > len(current): break? Actually when n >= len(current), we cannot split further; algorithm terminates (current is 1-minimal). But need to ensure that no further reduction is possible. So condition: if n >= len(current), break.\n  - Partition current into n subsets. How to partition? As evenly as possible. For ddmin, subsets are contiguous? Usually ddmin uses subsets of the configuration in order (the configuration is an ordered sequence). The \"ordered configuration\" suggests items are in a specific order. The problem statement says \"ordered configuration\". So we need to treat items as a list in given order. Partition into n contiguous subsets (like splits). For ddmin, typical implementation: split into n subsets of approximately equal size. Subset i: items from start + i*size to start + (i+1)*size, except last may be larger. But the spec says \"Partition at the current granularity and test subsets before complements.\" So we partition the current configuration (ordered list) into n subsets (contiguous). Then test each subset individually. For each subset, run test. If outcome is FAIL, set current = subset, n = 2, reset and continue loop (break out of subset testing). If no subset FAIL, then test complements of each subset? The complement is the set of items not in the subset. But note: the complement may overlap across subsets? Actually each subset's complement is everything else in current. But we should test each complement in order? The spec says \"test subsets before complements.\" So after all subsets are tested (none FAIL), we test complements in same order. For each complement, if outcome is FAIL, set current = complement, n = 2, reset and continue. If no FAIL from complements either, then double n (n = min(2*n, len(current))) and continue loop (if n <= len(current); otherwise break).\n\nEdge: when testing a complement, we must ensure it's not the same as current? But complement is always smaller than current (since subset nonempty? Actually if subset is empty, not allowed. Partition ensures each subset has at least one item? For n=2, subsets each have at least 1 if length≥2. So complement is also smaller. So acceptable.\n\nAfter loop, current is the 1-minimal failure configuration. Then return result: {\"minimal_failure\": current, \"test_count\": tests, \"terminal_outcome\": \"FAIL\"}? But spec says \"return the 1-minimal failure, test count, and terminal outcome.\" And \"Return only JSON-serializable values with the exact named fields.\" So we need to decide field names. Possibly \"minimal_failure\", \"test_count\", \"terminal_outcome\". Or maybe \"one_minimal_failure\"? The spec says \"1-minimal failure\". Let's use \"minimal_failure\", \"test_count\", \"terminal_outcome\". Also ensure types: minimal_failure is list of strings from items, test_count int, terminal_outcome string \"FAIL\".\n\nBut also need to handle case where initial configuration is not FAIL: raise ValueError.\n\nAlso need to ensure we do not mutate case. Use copy.\n\nImplementation details: Use a helper function to test config. Use list of items as strings. Use recursion? But iterative is fine.\n\nBe careful about indexing and partition sizes. For partition into n subsets, we can compute size = len(current) // n, remainder = len(current) % n. Then create subsets: for i in range(n): start = i*size + min(i, remainder), end = start + size + (1 if i < remainder else 0). Slicing.\n\nThen test each subset: call test(subset). For complement, we can compute complement as current[:start] + current[end:].\n\nKeep track of test count; each call to test increments count.\n\nPotential edge: when subset or complement is empty? Not possible because n <= len(current) and each subset gets at least one item? Actually if len(current) < n we break, so len(current) >= n. But if n==len(current), then each subset is size 1, remainder 0. So subsets are singletons, complement of a singleton is list of all other items (size len-1). All fine.\n\nTermination: when n >= len(current), break. But also if no FAIL after doubling and n >= len(current), break.\n\nWe must ensure that after reducing to a subset, we reset n to 2 and continue. That is typical.\n\nNow write the solve function. Return dict with JSON keys.\n\nMake sure to import nothing extra.\n\nNow, the response must be a JSON object with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source string. So we need to produce a JSON string that itself contains the Python source as a string. So we need to escape newlines, backslashes, quotes. We'll write the Python code as a string in the JSON.\n\nI'll write the solve function in a code block but then escape.\n\nLet me construct the Python source:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    import copy\n    \n    # Validate payload\n    payload = case.get('payload')\n    if not payload:\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing payload\")\n    items = payload.get('items')\n    required_failure = payload.get('required_failure_items')\n    unresolved_without = payload.get('unresolved_without')\n    if not (isinstance(items, list) and isinstance(required_failure, list) and isinstance(unresolved_without, list)):\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid payload structure\")\n    \n    # Define oracle\n    def outcome(config):\n        # config is list of items (in order)\n        # Check unresolved condition first? According to spec: \"unresolved_without\" means if those items are missing, outcome UNRESOLVED.\n        # Determine if any unresolved_without item is missing from config.\n        missing_unresolved = [x for x in unresolved_without if x not in config]\n        if missing_unresolved:\n            return 'UNRESOLVED'\n        # Check if all required_failure items are present\n        missing_required = [x for x in required_failure if x not in config]\n        if not missing_required:\n            return 'FAIL'\n        else:\n            return 'PASS'\n    \n    # Initial configuration must be FAIL\n    initial = list(items)  # ordered list\n    if outcome(initial) != 'FAIL':\n        raise ValueError(\"Initial configuration does not produce FAIL\")\n    \n    # ddmin algorithm\n    current = initial[:]\n    n = 2\n    test_count = 0\n    \n    # Helper to test and count\n    def test(config):\n        nonlocal test_count\n        test_count += 1\n        return outcome(config)\n    \n    while True:\n        if n >= len(current):\n            break\n        # Partition into n subsets\n        length = len(current)\n        size = length // n\n        remainder = length % n\n        subsets = []\n        start = 0\n        for i in range(n):\n            subset_size = size + (1 if i < remainder else 0)\n            end = start + subset_size\n            subsets.append(current[start:end])\n            start = end\n        # Test subsets first\n        found = False\n        for subset in subsets:\n            if test(subset) == 'FAIL':\n                current = subset[:]\n                n = 2\n                found = True\n                break\n        if found:\n            continue\n        # Test complements\n        start = 0\n        for i in range(n):\n            subset_size = size + (1 if i < remainder else 0)\n            end = start + subset_size\n            complement = current[:start] + current[end:]\n            if test(complement) == 'FAIL':\n                current = complement[:]\n                n = 2\n                found = True\n                break\n            start = end\n        if found:\n            continue\n        # No FAIL found, double granularity\n        n = min(2 * n, length)\n        # if n reaches length, loop will break next iteration\n    \n    # After loop, current is 1-minimal failure\n    return {\n        \"minimal_failure\": current,\n        \"test_count\": test_count,\n        \"terminal_outcome\": \"FAIL\"\n    }\n```\n\nBut need to ensure the oracle respects the order of items? The outcome does not depend on order, only set membership. That's fine.\n\nNow we need to produce the JSON with \"implementation\" key. We'll escape the Python string: replace backslashes with double backslashes, double quotes with backslash-quote, newlines with \\n. Then put into JSON object.\n\nAlso note: The problem statement mentions \"registered procedure task SE-DD-01\" and \"fixture_payload\" shows cases. But we are just implementing solve(case). So code above should be correct.\n\nDouble-check: The algorithm as described: \"Decrease granularity after reduction and otherwise double it up to current size.\" That's what we did: after reduction, set n=2. If no reduction, double n (but not exceed current size). Also \"Only FAIL permits reduction; PASS and UNRESOLVED do not.\" That's satisfied. \"Test subsets before complements.\" That's satisfied. The algorithm also says \"Partition at the current granularity\". We partition into n subsets.\n\nPotential edge: What if a subset is the same as current? That would only happen if n=1, but we start at 2 and increase, so not. Also complements could be same as current if subset empty, but not possible.\n\nNow ensure the final result is 1-minimal: The algorithm's termination ensures that no single removal (i.e., n>=length) yields FAIL. That is 1-minimal.\n\nNow produce the JSON response.\n\nI'll write the Python code as a string with proper escaping. But careful: The code includes triple-quoted strings? No. Use single quotes inside. Also there are backslashes in the escape for dictionary? None.\n\nLet me manually escape the string:\n\n- Replace all backslashes with \\\\ (though none in code except maybe in string, but we don't have literals with backslash).\n- Replace double quotes with \\\".\n- Replace newlines with \\n.\n\nI'll write the code as a single line with \\n escapes.\n\nBut easier: I can construct the JSON manually by writing the Python code as a multiline string and then using json.dumps. But since I'm writing output directly, I need to ensure the JSON is valid. I'll do it step by step.\n\nAlternatively, I can write the Python code as a raw string literal, but JSON requires escaping. I'll produce the JSON string with escaped characters.\n\nI'll write the implementation value as a string that looks like:\n\n\"def solve(case):\\n    import copy\\n    ...\"\n\nLet me write the final JSON object:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    import copy\\n    ...\"}\